#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262789 :question/problem "![](images/eb/ebef81911a434828ab94e643007d06b677538cdf0995198c7a065e35a8f81996.png)\n\nFind $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for the ellipse shown above." :question/worked-solution "Since our ellipse is centered at $(-1,-1)$ and has semiaxes $2$ and $1,$ its equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-(-1))^{2}}{{2}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-(-1))^{2}}{{1}^{2}}=1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{4}+(y+1)^{2}=1.\n$$\n\nNow, to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we use implicit differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{4}+(y+1)^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(1) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{4}\\right)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}((y+1)^{2}) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{2(x+1)}{4}+2(y+1)\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =0 \\\\ 2(y+1)\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{x+1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{x+1}{4(y+1)}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262790 :question/problem "![](images/eb/eb0fbc8f57621946a5ec5168e8c607d230269e0c6a64b4c86c835c110f0f6288.png)\n\nFind $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for the ellipse shown above." :question/worked-solution "Since our ellipse is centered at $(0,1)$ and has semiaxes $2$ and $3,$ its equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-0)^{2}}{{2}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{{3}^{2}}=1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{9}=1.\n$$\n\nNow, to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we use implicit differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{9}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(1) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}\\right)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{9}\\right) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{2x}{4}+\\frac{2(y-1)}{9}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{2(y-1)}{9}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{x}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{9x}{4(y-1)}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262791 :question/problem "![](images/e4/e4c39b409f6049a4fdead506c329d232820876c37ede9540adfeade2d7c1b9dc.png)\n\nFind $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for the ellipse shown above." :question/worked-solution "Since our ellipse is centered at $(3,0)$ and has semiaxes $3$ and $2,$ its equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{{3}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-0)^{2}}{{2}^{2}}=1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}=1.\n$$\n\nNow, to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we use implicit differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(1) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{9}\\right)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}\\right) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{2(x-3)}{9}+\\frac{2y}{4}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{y}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{2(x-3)}{9} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{4(x-3)}{9y}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262794 :question/problem "![](images/16/162c0d0310cbbb533c30af373820751fc6c5a447127958486571224d7f8ac07a.png)\n\nFind the arc length of the curve $\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}t,\\hspace{0.167em}{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}t⟩$ from $t=0$ to $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)$ and $‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}t),\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{e}^{t}(\\operatorname{cos}t-\\operatorname{sin}t),\\hspace{0.167em}{e}^{t}(\\operatorname{cos}t+\\operatorname{sin}t)⟩ \\\\  & ={e}^{t}⟨\\operatorname{cos}t-\\operatorname{sin}t,\\hspace{0.167em}\\operatorname{cos}t+\\operatorname{sin}t⟩ \\\\ ‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖ & =|{e}^{t}|\\sqrt{{(\\operatorname{cos}t-\\operatorname{sin}t)}^{2}+{(\\operatorname{cos}t+\\operatorname{sin}t)}^{2}} \\\\  & ={e}^{t}\\sqrt{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t-2\\operatorname{cos}t\\operatorname{sin}t+{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t+{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t+2\\operatorname{cos}t\\operatorname{sin}t+{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t} \\\\  & ={e}^{t}\\sqrt{2({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t+{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length of the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{2}{e}^{t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{e}^{t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}{e}^{t}\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}({e}^{\\pi /2}-{e}^{0}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}({e}^{\\pi /2}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262796 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{vmatrix}5 & -9 \\\\ -3 & 7\\end{vmatrix}=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a $2\\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{vmatrix}=ad-bc.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}5 & -9 \\\\ -3 & 7\\end{vmatrix} & =5\\cdot 7-(-9)\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =35-27 \\\\  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262797 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -10 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{vmatrix}=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a $2\\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{vmatrix}=ad-bc.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -10 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{vmatrix} & =-1\\cdot 5-(-10)\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =-5-20 \\\\  & =-25\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262798 :question/problem "![](images/c4/c4aa91668440687bbae4b1f85672f5cbaf7ce00d978049c7558eed99b7361d1a.png)\n\nFind the arc length of the curve $\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨3t-{t}^{3},\\hspace{0.167em}3{t}^{2}⟩$ from $t=-1$ to $t=1.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)$ and $‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t-{t}^{3}),\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{t}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3-3{t}^{2},\\hspace{0.167em}6t⟩ \\\\ ‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(3-3{t}^{2})}^{2}+{(6t)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9-18{t}^{2}+9{t}^{4}+36{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9{t}^{4}+18{t}^{2}+9} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+2{t}^{2}+1} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{({t}^{2}+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =3({t}^{2}+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length of the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}3({t}^{2}+1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{-1}^{1}({t}^{2}+1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =3(\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}+t){|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =3(\\frac{{1}^{3}}{3}+1-[\\frac{(-1)^{3}}{3}+(-1)]) \\\\  & =3(\\frac{1}{3}+1+\\frac{1}{3}+1) \\\\  & =3\\left(\\frac{8}{3}\\right) \\\\  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262800 :question/problem "Calculate $det(C)$, if\n$$\n\\displaystyle C=\\begin{bmatrix}-x & -2y \\\\ \\frac{x}{2} & 5y\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a $2\\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{vmatrix}=ad-bc.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(C) & =\\begin{vmatrix}-x & -2y \\\\ \\frac{x}{2} & 5y\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-x\\cdot 5y-(-2y)\\cdot \\frac{x}{2} \\\\  & =-5xy+xy \\\\  & =-4xy\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262801 :question/problem "![](images/33/33412629fc30584cf4a1726eef7e4f23f623ae4439d25045d61e36f68914dacd.png)\n\nFind the arc length of the curve $\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨t,\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{1}{3}({t}^{2}+2)^{3/2}⟩$ from $t=-1$ to $t=2.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)$ and $‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t),\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{3}({t}^{2}+2)^{3/2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.167em}t\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+2}⟩ \\\\ ‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{(t\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}({t}^{2}+2)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+2{t}^{2}+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{({t}^{2}+1)^{2}} \\\\  & ={t}^{2}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length of the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}({t}^{2}+1)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}+t){|}_{-1}^{2} \\\\  & =(\\frac{{2}^{3}}{3}+2)-(\\frac{(-1)^{3}}{3}+(-1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}+2+\\frac{1}{3}+1 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262802 :question/problem "Calculate $det(D)$, if\n$$\n\\displaystyle D=\\begin{bmatrix}2x & y \\\\ -5 & xy\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a $2\\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{vmatrix}=ad-bc.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(D) & =\\begin{vmatrix}2x & y \\\\ -5 & xy\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2x\\cdot xy-y\\cdot (-5) \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}y+5y \\\\  & =y(2{x}^{2}+5)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262803 :question/problem "If\n$$\n\\displaystyle U=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -5 \\\\ 2k & -k+2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nand\n$\\text{det}(U)=-1$, what is the value of $k?$" :question/worked-solution "For a $2\\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{vmatrix}=ad-bc.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(U) & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -5 \\\\ 2k & -k+2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3\\cdot (-k+2)-(-5)\\cdot 2k \\\\  & =7k+6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(U)=-1$, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(U) & =-1 \\\\ 7k+6 & =-1 \\\\ 7k & =-1-6 \\\\ 7k & =-7 \\\\ k & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262804 :question/problem "If\n$$\n\\displaystyle T=\\begin{bmatrix}v & 2v \\\\ 2 & v+8\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nand $det(T)=-4$, what is the value of $v?$" :question/worked-solution "For a $2\\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{vmatrix}=ad-bc.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(T) & =\\begin{vmatrix}v & 2v \\\\ 2 & v+8\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =v\\cdot (v+8)-2v\\cdot 2 \\\\  & ={v}^{2}+4v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(T)=-4$, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(T) & =-4 \\\\ {v}^{2}+4v & =-4 \\\\ {v}^{2}+4v+4 & =0 \\\\ (v+2)^{2} & =0 \\\\ v & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262805 :question/problem "If\n$$\n\\displaystyle P=\\begin{bmatrix}r-3 & r \\\\ 3 & r\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nand $det(P)=-9$, what is the value of $r?$" :question/worked-solution "For a $2\\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{vmatrix}=ad-bc.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(P) & =\\begin{vmatrix}r-3 & r \\\\ 3 & r\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(r-3)\\cdot r-r\\cdot 3 \\\\  & ={r}^{2}-6r\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(P)=-9$, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(P) & =-9 \\\\ {r}^{2}-6r & =-9 \\\\ {r}^{2}-6r+9 & =0 \\\\ (r-3)^{2} & =0 \\\\ r & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262806 :question/problem "If\n$$\n\\displaystyle B=\\begin{bmatrix}5-p & p \\\\ 3 & p\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nand $det(B)=1$, what is the value of $p?$" :question/worked-solution "For a $2\\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{vmatrix}=ad-bc.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(B) & =\\begin{vmatrix}5-p & p \\\\ 3 & p\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(5-p)\\cdot p-p\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =-{p}^{2}+2p\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(B)=1$, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(B) & =1 \\\\ -{p}^{2}+2p & =1 \\\\ -{p}^{2}+2p-1 & =0 \\\\ {p}^{2}-2p+1 & =0 \\\\ (p-1)^{2} & =0 \\\\ p & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262807 :question/problem "If\n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}3y & y \\\\ 3 & y-5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nand $det(A)=-27$, what is the value of $y?$" :question/worked-solution "For a $2\\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{vmatrix}=ad-bc.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =\\begin{vmatrix}3y & y \\\\ 3 & y-5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3y\\cdot (y-5)-y\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =3{y}^{2}-18y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(A)=-27$, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =-27 \\\\ 3{y}^{2}-18y & =-27 \\\\ 3{y}^{2}-18y+27 & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2}-6y+9 & =0 \\\\ (y-3)^{2} & =0 \\\\ y & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262810 :question/problem "Find the arc length of the curve $\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨2t,\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{1}{3}{t}^{3},\\hspace{0.167em}{t}^{2}⟩$ from $t=-1$ to $t=0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)$ and $‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t),\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{3}{t}^{3}),\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,\\hspace{0.167em}{t}^{2},\\hspace{0.167em}2t⟩ \\\\ ‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(2)}^{2}+{({t}^{2})}^{2}+{(2t)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+4{t}^{2}+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{({t}^{2}+2)^{2}} \\\\  & ={t}^{2}+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length of the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}({t}^{2}+2)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{3}{t}^{3}+2t){|}_{-1}^{0} \\\\  & =(\\frac{{0}^{3}}{3}+2(0))-(\\frac{(-1)^{3}}{3}+2(-1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}+2 \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262836 :question/problem "Calculate the area of the $△\\,ABC,$ if $A(5,0,5),$ $B(6,-1,4),$ $C(9,1,0).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a} \\\\  & =(6\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j}+4\\mathbf{k})-(5\\mathbf{i}+5\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j}-\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨1,-1,-1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =\\mathbf{c}-\\mathbf{a} \\\\  & =(9\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j})-(5\\mathbf{i}+5\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =4\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}-5\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨4,1,-5⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula to compute the cross product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 1 & -5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -5\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -5\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =((-1)\\cdot (-5)-1\\cdot (-1))\\mathbf{i}-(1\\cdot (-5)-4\\cdot (-1))\\mathbf{j}+(1\\cdot 1-4\\cdot (-1))\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =6\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}+5\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨6,1,5⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{6}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{5}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{62}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,ABC$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{\\sqrt{62}}{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262845 :question/problem "Calculate the area of the $△\\,PQR,$ if $P(2,6,6),$ $Q(4,8,2),$ $R(0,5,6).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{PR}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =\\mathbf{q}-\\mathbf{p} \\\\  & =(4\\mathbf{i}+8\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k})-(2\\mathbf{i}+6\\mathbf{j}+6\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =2\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j}-4\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨2,2,-4⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{PR} & =\\mathbf{r}-\\mathbf{p} \\\\  & =(5\\mathbf{j}+6\\mathbf{k})-(2\\mathbf{i}+6\\mathbf{j}+6\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =-2\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =⟨-2,-1,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula to compute the cross product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\times \\overset{\\to }{PR} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 2 & 2 & -4 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -4 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -4 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(2\\cdot 0-(-1)\\cdot (-4))\\mathbf{i}-(2\\cdot 0-(-2)\\cdot (-4))\\mathbf{j}+(2\\cdot (-1)-(-2)\\cdot 2)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =-4\\mathbf{i}+8\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-4,8,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{PR}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\times \\overset{\\to }{PR}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-4)^{2}+{8}^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{84} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{21}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,PQR$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{2\\sqrt{21}}{2}=\\sqrt{21}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262848 :question/problem "Calculate the area of the $△\\,PQR,$ if $P(6,1,5),$ $Q(5,1,7),$ $R(4,2,0).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{PR}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =\\mathbf{q}-\\mathbf{p} \\\\  & =(5\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}+7\\mathbf{k})-(6\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}+5\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =-\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-1,0,2⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{PR} & =\\mathbf{r}-\\mathbf{p} \\\\  & =(4\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j})-(6\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}+5\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =-2\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}-5\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-2,1,-5⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula to compute the cross product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\times \\overset{\\to }{PR} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -5\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -5\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(0\\cdot (-5)-1\\cdot 2)\\mathbf{i}-((-1)\\cdot (-5)-(-2)\\cdot 2)\\mathbf{j}+((-1)\\cdot 1-(-2)\\cdot 0)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =-2\\mathbf{i}-9\\mathbf{j}-\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-2,-9,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{PR}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\times \\overset{\\to }{PR}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+(-9)^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{86}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,PQR$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{\\sqrt{86}}{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262849 :question/problem "Calculate the area of the $△\\,WXY,$ if $W(3,9,4),$ $X(1,7,3),$ $Y(6,12,8).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{WX}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{WY}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{WX} & =\\mathbf{x}-\\mathbf{w} \\\\  & =(\\mathbf{i}+7\\mathbf{j}+3\\mathbf{k})-(3\\mathbf{i}+9\\mathbf{j}+4\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =-2\\mathbf{i}-2\\mathbf{j}-\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-2,-2,-1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{WY} & =\\mathbf{y}-\\mathbf{w} \\\\  & =(6\\mathbf{i}+12\\mathbf{j}+8\\mathbf{k})-(3\\mathbf{i}+9\\mathbf{j}+4\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =3\\mathbf{i}+3\\mathbf{j}+4\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨3,3,4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula to compute the cross product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{WX}\\times \\overset{\\to }{WY} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ -2 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 3 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =((-2)\\cdot 4-3\\cdot (-1))\\mathbf{i}-((-2)\\cdot 4-3\\cdot (-1))\\mathbf{j}+((-2)\\cdot 3-3\\cdot (-2))\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =-5\\mathbf{i}+5\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =⟨-5,5,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{WX}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{WY}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{WX}\\times \\overset{\\to }{WY}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-5)^{2}+{5}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =5\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,WXY$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262863 :question/problem "Calculate the length of the altitude $CH$ of the $△\\,ABC,$ if $A(4,-1,1),$ $B(0,-3,4),$ $C(2,1,4).$" :question/worked-solution "Note that the length of the altitude $CH$ can be related to the area of $△\\,ABC$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot AB\n$$\n\nSo, to find $CH,$ we first find ${A}_{△}.$ To start, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a} \\\\  & =(-3\\mathbf{j}+4\\mathbf{k})-(4\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =-4\\mathbf{i}-2\\mathbf{j}+3\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-4,-2,3⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =\\mathbf{c}-\\mathbf{a} \\\\  & =(2\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}+4\\mathbf{k})-(4\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =-2\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j}+3\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-2,2,3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ -4 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}-4 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}-4 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =((-2)\\cdot 3-2\\cdot 3)\\mathbf{i}-((-4)\\cdot 3-(-2)\\cdot 3)\\mathbf{j}+((-4)\\cdot 2-(-2)\\cdot (-2))\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =-12\\mathbf{i}+6\\mathbf{j}-12\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-12,6,-12⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-12)^{2}+{6}^{2}+(-12)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{324} \\\\  & =18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,ABC$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{18}{2}=9.\n$$\n\nLet's also compute $AB$ since it appears in the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-4)^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{29}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, using the formula ${A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot AB,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△} & =\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot AB \\\\ 9 & =\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot \\sqrt{29} \\\\ 18 & =CH\\cdot \\sqrt{29} \\\\ CH & =\\frac{18}{\\sqrt{29}} \\\\ CH & =\\frac{18\\sqrt{29}}{29}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262864 :question/problem "Calculate the length of the altitude $RH$ of the $△\\,PRQ,$ if $P(3,0,4),$ $Q(2,3,1),$ $R(0,3,1).$" :question/worked-solution "Note that the length of the altitude $RH$ can be related to the area of $△\\,PQR$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}RH\\cdot PQ\n$$\n\nSo, to find $RH,$ we first find ${A}_{△}.$ To start, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{PR}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =\\mathbf{q}-\\mathbf{p} \\\\  & =(2\\mathbf{i}+3\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k})-(3\\mathbf{i}+4\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =-\\mathbf{i}+3\\mathbf{j}-3\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =⟨-1,3,-3⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{PR} & =\\mathbf{r}-\\mathbf{p} \\\\  & =(3\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k})-(3\\mathbf{i}+4\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =-3\\mathbf{i}+3\\mathbf{j}-3\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-3,3,-3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\times \\overset{\\to }{PR} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ -1 & 3 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 3 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -3 \\\\ 3 & -3\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -3 \\\\ -3 & -3\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 3\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(3\\cdot (-3)-3\\cdot (-3))\\mathbf{i}-((-1)\\cdot (-3)-(-3)\\cdot (-3))\\mathbf{j}+((-1)\\cdot 3-(-3)\\cdot 3)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =6\\mathbf{j}+6\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨0,6,6⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{PR}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\times \\overset{\\to }{PR}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{6}^{2}+{6}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{72} \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,PQR$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{6\\sqrt{2}}{2}=3\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nLet's also compute $PQ$ since it appears in the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}PQ & =|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{3}^{2}+(-3)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{19}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, using the formula ${A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}RH\\cdot PQ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△} & =\\frac{1}{2}RH\\cdot PQ \\\\ 3\\sqrt{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}RH\\cdot \\sqrt{19} \\\\ 6\\sqrt{2} & =RH\\cdot \\sqrt{19} \\\\ RH & =\\frac{6\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{19}} \\\\ RH & =\\frac{6\\sqrt{38}}{19}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262865 :question/problem "Calculate the length of the altitude $MH$ of the $△\\,LMN,$ if $L(-1,4,1),$ $M(0,3,4),$ $N(1,4,2).$" :question/worked-solution "Note that the length of the altitude $MH$ can be related to the area of $△\\,LMN$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}MH\\cdot LN\n$$\n\nSo, to find $MH,$ we first find ${A}_{△}.$ To start, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{LM}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{LN}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{LM} & =\\mathbf{m}-\\mathbf{l} \\\\  & =(3\\mathbf{j}+4\\mathbf{k})-(-\\mathbf{i}+4\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j}+3\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨1,-1,3⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{LN} & =\\mathbf{n}-\\mathbf{l} \\\\  & =(\\mathbf{i}+4\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k})-(-\\mathbf{i}+4\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =2\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨2,0,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{LM}\\times \\overset{\\to }{LN} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 1 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =((-1)\\cdot 1-0\\cdot 3)\\mathbf{i}-(1\\cdot 1-2\\cdot 3)\\mathbf{j}+(1\\cdot 0-2\\cdot (-1))\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =-\\mathbf{i}+5\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-1,5,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{LM}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{LN}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{LM}\\times \\overset{\\to }{LN}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{5}^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{30}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,LMN$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{\\sqrt{30}}{2}.\n$$\n\nLet's also compute $LN$ since it appears in the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}LN & =|\\overset{\\to }{LN}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, using the formula ${A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}MH\\cdot LN,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△} & =\\frac{1}{2}MH\\cdot LN \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{30}}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}MH\\cdot \\sqrt{5} \\\\ \\sqrt{30} & =MH\\cdot \\sqrt{5} \\\\ MH & =\\frac{\\sqrt{30}}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ MH & =\\sqrt{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262873 :question/problem "Find $\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c})$ given that $\\mathbf{a}=⟨-1,1,0⟩$ and $\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}=⟨6,1,7⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "Substituting given vectors into the formula for the scalar triple product, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =⟨-1,1,0⟩\\cdot ⟨6,1,7⟩ \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot 6+1\\cdot 1+0\\cdot 7 \\\\  & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262874 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{a}=⟨0,9,3⟩,$ $\\mathbf{b}=⟨3,2,0⟩$ and $\\mathbf{c}=⟨-1,1,1⟩.$ Find $\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}).$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the scalar triple product $\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c})$ is just the determinant of the matrix whose rows are given by $\\mathbf{a},$ $\\mathbf{b},$ and $\\mathbf{c}.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 9 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}-9\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}+3\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =0-9(3\\cdot 1-(-1)\\cdot 0)+3(3\\cdot 1-(-1)\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =0-27+15 \\\\  & =-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262878 :question/problem "Find the value of $(\\mathbf{f}(t)-3\\mathbf{g}(t))^{'}$ at $t=0,$ if \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{f}(t) & =⟨2\\sqrt{t+1},\\hspace{0.167em}{t}^{3},\\hspace{0.167em}\\operatorname{ln}(t+1)⟩, \\\\ \\mathbf{g}(t) & =⟨\\frac{1}{3(t+1)},\\hspace{0.167em}\\operatorname{sin}t,\\hspace{0.167em}\\operatorname{tan}t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that\n\n$$\n(\\mathbf{f}(t)-3\\mathbf{g}(t))^{'}={\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t)-3{\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t).\n$$\n\nLet's now find the derivatives of each function.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\sqrt{t+1}),\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}),\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}(t+1))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t+1}},\\hspace{0.167em}3{t}^{2},\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{1}{t+1}⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{1}{3(t+1)}\\right),\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}t),\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{tan}t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{1}{3(t+1)^{2}},\\hspace{0.167em}\\operatorname{cos}t,\\hspace{0.167em}{\\operatorname{sec}}^{2}t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf{f}(0)-3\\mathbf{g}(0))^{'} & ={\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(0)-3{\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(0) \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{0+1}},\\hspace{0.167em}3(0)^{2},\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{1}{0+1}⟩-3⟨-\\frac{1}{3(0+1)^{2}},\\hspace{0.167em}\\operatorname{cos}0,\\hspace{0.167em}{\\operatorname{sec}}^{2}0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,0,1⟩-3⟨-\\frac{1}{3},1,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,0,1⟩-⟨-1,3,3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,-3,-2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262882 :question/problem "Find the value of $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -3 & 1\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -3 & 1\\end{vmatrix} & =-2\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{vmatrix}-1\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{vmatrix}+1\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ 5 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-2(4-0)-1(1-0)-1(-3-20) \\\\  & =-2\\cdot 4-1\\cdot 1+1\\cdot (-23) \\\\  & =-8-1-23 \\\\  & =-32\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262883 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{p}=⟨7,4,9⟩,$ $\\mathbf{q}=⟨1,2,-2⟩$ and $\\mathbf{r}=⟨3,0,5⟩.$ Find $\\mathbf{q}\\cdot (\\mathbf{r}\\times \\mathbf{p}).$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the scalar triple product $\\mathbf{q}\\cdot (\\mathbf{r}\\times \\mathbf{p})$ is just the determinant of the matrix whose rows are given by $\\mathbf{q},$ $\\mathbf{r},$ and $\\mathbf{p}.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{q}\\cdot (\\mathbf{r}\\times \\mathbf{p}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 7 & 4 & 9\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 9\\end{vmatrix}-2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 5 \\\\ 7 & 9\\end{vmatrix}+(-2)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(0\\cdot 9-4\\cdot 5)-2(3\\cdot 9-7\\cdot 5)-2(3\\cdot 4-7\\cdot 0) \\\\  & =-20+16-24 \\\\  & =-28.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262884 :question/problem "Find $\\mathbf{c}\\cdot (\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}),$ if $\\mathbf{a}\\cdot ((-3\\mathbf{b})\\times (-7\\mathbf{c}))=7.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor out the constants and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot ((-3\\mathbf{b})\\times (-7\\mathbf{c})) & =7 \\\\ (-3)\\cdot (-7)\\cdot [\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c})] & =7 \\\\ \\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we swap $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{c}$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{c}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{a}) & =-\\frac{1}{3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand again we swap $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{c}\\cdot (\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}) & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262885 :question/problem "Find $\\mathbf{b}\\cdot (\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{c}),$ if $\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}=⟨2,5,1⟩$ and $\\mathbf{a}=⟨6,1,-9⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we compute the triple scalar product \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =⟨6,1,-9⟩\\cdot ⟨2,5,1⟩ \\\\  & =6\\cdot 2+1\\cdot 5+(-9)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =12+5-9 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we swap $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{b}\\cdot (\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{c}) &=-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262886 :question/problem "Find $\\mathbf{c}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{a}),$ if $\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}=⟨6,-1,3⟩$ and $\\mathbf{a}=⟨3,5,-4⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we compute the triple scalar product \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =⟨3,5,-4⟩\\cdot ⟨6,-1,3⟩ \\\\  & =3\\cdot 6+5\\cdot (-1)+(-4)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =18-5-12 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we swap $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{c}$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{c}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{a}) &=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262892 :question/problem "Calculate the volume of the parallelepiped spanned by $\\mathbf{p}=⟨2,2,3⟩,$ $\\mathbf{q}=⟨4,2,-1⟩,$ and $\\mathbf{r}=⟨-5,0,3⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "The volume of the parallelepiped spanned by three vectors is given by the absolute value of their triple product.\n\nFirst, we compute the triple product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{p}\\cdot (\\mathbf{q}\\times \\mathbf{r}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -5 & 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix}-2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}4 & -1 \\\\ -5 & 3\\end{vmatrix}+3\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ -5 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2(2\\cdot 3-0\\cdot (-1))-2(4\\cdot 3-(-5)\\cdot (-1))+3(4\\cdot 0-(-5)\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =12-14+30 \\\\  & =28\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the absolute value. So the volume of the corresponding parallelepiped is\n\n$$\nV=|28|=28.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262893 :question/problem "Calculate the volume of the parallelepiped spanned by $\\mathbf{a}=⟨0,-1,4⟩,$ $\\mathbf{b}=⟨3,9,2⟩,$ and $\\mathbf{c}=⟨2,7,6⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "The volume of the parallelepiped spanned by three vectors is given by the absolute value of their triple product.\n\nFirst, we compute the triple product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -1 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 9 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 7 & 6\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}9 & 2 \\\\ 7 & 6\\end{vmatrix}-(-1)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 6\\end{vmatrix}+4\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 9 \\\\ 2 & 7\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =0+(3\\cdot 6-2\\cdot 2)+4(3\\cdot 7-2\\cdot 9) \\\\  & =14+12 \\\\  & =26\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nFinally, we take the absolute value. So the volume of the corresponding parallelepiped is\n\n$$\nV=|26|=26.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262894 :question/problem "Find $k$ given that the vectors $\\mathbf{a}=⟨k,1,4⟩,$ $\\mathbf{b}=⟨8,0,3⟩,$ and $\\mathbf{c}=⟨3,1,1⟩$ are coplanar." :question/worked-solution "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}k & 1 & 4 \\\\ 8 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =k\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}-1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}8 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{vmatrix}+4\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}8 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =k(0\\cdot 1-1\\cdot 3)-(8\\cdot 1-3\\cdot 3)+4(8\\cdot 1-3\\cdot 0) \\\\  & =-3k+1+32 \\\\  & =33-3k \\\\  & =3(11-k).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =0 \\\\ 3(11-k) & =0 \\\\ 11-k & =0 \\\\ k & =11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $k=11.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262895 :question/problem "Determine the equation of the level curve that passes through the point $P(1,1)$ for the function $f(x,y)=xy-2.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the value of the function at $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,1) & =1(1)-2 \\\\  & =1-2 \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the level curve that passes through $P(1,1)$ is the $-1$-level curve.\n\nRecall that the level curves for the function $f(x,y)$ are given by the equation\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=c.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're interested in $c=-1.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =c \\\\ xy-2 & =-1 \\\\ xy & =1 \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the level curve that passes through $P(1,1)$ is the hyperbola $y=\\frac{1}{x}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262896 :question/problem "Determine the equation of the level curve that passes through the point $P(2,0)$ for the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{1}{x-{y}^{2}+3}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the value of the function at $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2,0) & =\\frac{1}{2-{0}^{2}+3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2-0+3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the level curve that passes through $P(2,0)$ is the $\\frac{1}{5}$-level curve.\n\nRecall that the level curves for the function $f(x,y)$ are given by the equation\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=c.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're interested in $c=\\frac{1}{5}.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =c \\\\ \\frac{1}{x-{y}^{2}+3} & =\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ 5 & =x-{y}^{2}+3 \\\\ x & ={y}^{2}+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the level curve that passes through $P(2,0)$ is the parabola $x={y}^{2}+2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262897 :question/problem "Find the value of $(2\\mathbf{f}(t)-\\mathbf{g}(t))^{'}$ at $t=-1,$ if \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{f}(t) & =⟨8\\sqrt{3-t},\\hspace{0.167em}\\operatorname{sin}(\\pi t),\\hspace{0.167em}\\operatorname{ln}(2t)⟩, \\\\ \\mathbf{g}(t) & =⟨{t}^{2}-t,\\hspace{0.167em}\\pi {t}^{2},\\hspace{0.167em}1-{t}^{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that\n\n$$\n(2\\mathbf{f}(t)-\\mathbf{g}(t))^{'}=2{\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t)-{\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t).\n$$\n\nLet's now find the derivatives of each function.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(8\\sqrt{3-t}),\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}(\\pi t)),\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}(2t))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{3-t}},\\hspace{0.167em}\\pi \\operatorname{cos}(\\pi t),\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{1}{t}⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-t),\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\pi {t}^{2}),\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-{t}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2t-1,\\hspace{0.167em}2\\pi t,\\hspace{0.167em}-2t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2\\mathbf{f}(-1)-\\mathbf{g}(-1))^{'} & =2{\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(-1)-{\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(-1) \\\\  & =2⟨-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{3-(-1)}},\\hspace{0.167em}\\pi \\operatorname{cos}(-\\pi ),\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{1}{(-1)}⟩-⟨2(-1)-1,\\hspace{0.167em}2\\pi (-1),\\hspace{0.167em}-2(-1)⟩ \\\\  & =2⟨-2,-\\pi ,-1⟩-⟨-3,-2\\pi ,2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-4,-2\\pi ,-2⟩-⟨-3,-2\\pi ,2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,0,-4⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262898 :question/problem "Find $t$ given that the vectors $\\mathbf{p}=⟨t,t,1⟩,$ $\\mathbf{q}=⟨3,2,6⟩,$ and $\\mathbf{r}=⟨3,4,0⟩$ are coplanar." :question/worked-solution "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{p}\\cdot (\\mathbf{q}\\times \\mathbf{r}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}t & t & 1 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 4 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =t\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 6 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{vmatrix}-t\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{vmatrix}+1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =t(2\\cdot 0-4\\cdot 6)-t(3\\cdot 0-3\\cdot 6)+(3\\cdot 4-3\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =-24t+18t+6 \\\\  & =-6t+6 \\\\  & =6(1-t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{p}\\cdot (\\mathbf{q}\\times \\mathbf{r}) & =0 \\\\ 6(1-t) & =0 \\\\ 1-t & =0 \\\\ t & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $t=1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262899 :question/problem "Find $t$ given that the vectors $\\mathbf{a}=⟨3,-1,7⟩,$ $\\mathbf{b}=⟨t,4,t⟩,$ and $\\mathbf{c}=⟨2,6,8⟩$ are coplanar." :question/worked-solution "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -1 & 7 \\\\ t & 4 & t \\\\ 2 & 6 & 8\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}4 & t \\\\ 6 & 8\\end{vmatrix}-(-1)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}t & t \\\\ 2 & 8\\end{vmatrix}+7\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}t & 4 \\\\ 2 & 6\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3(4\\cdot 8-6\\cdot t)+(t\\cdot 8-2\\cdot t)+7(t\\cdot 6-2\\cdot 4) \\\\  & =96-18t+8t-2t+42t-56 \\\\  & =30t+40 \\\\  & =10(3t+4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =0 \\\\ 10(3t+4) & =0 \\\\ 3t+4 & =0 \\\\ t & =-\\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $t=-\\frac{4}{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262900 :question/problem "Find the value of $(2\\mathbf{f}(t)+5\\mathbf{g}(t))^{'}$ at $t=1,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{f}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}2\\sqrt{3+t} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}(t-1) \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{2em}\\mathbf{g}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}2t-3 \\\\ {t}^{2} \\\\ (t+1)^{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "By the sum rule, we have \n \n\n$$\n(\\mathbf{2}f(t)+5\\mathbf{g}(t))^{'}=2{\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t)+5{\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t).\n$$\n\nLet's now find the derivatives of each function.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}(2\\sqrt{3+t}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}(t-1)) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3+t}} \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}(t-1) \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t-3) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}((t+1)^{2})\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2t \\\\ 2(t+1)\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence , we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2\\mathbf{f}(1)+5\\mathbf{g}(1))^{'} & =2{\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(1)+5{\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(1) \\\\  & =2\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3+1}} \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}(1-1) \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+5\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2(1) \\\\ 2(1+1)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+5\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ 10 \\\\ 20\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}11 \\\\ 10 \\\\ 20\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
